# Normalize every text file to LF, both in the repository and in the working
# tree on all platforms. Before this, ~40% of tracked sources were committed
# with CRLF and 22 files had mixed endings, so editing the same unit on Linux
# and Windows produced whole-file phantom diffs. FPC, Lazarus and PowerShell
# all read LF fine on Windows, and there is no .bat/.cmd in the tree that would
# require CRLF. Contributors need no local core.autocrlf setting: this wins.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Lazarus binary artifacts. Auto-detection already classifies these, but state
# it so a stray text-looking header can never trigger EOL rewriting.
*.res binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary

# The rules below are redundant under the global one above, but are kept
# because their rationale must survive any future change to that default.

# Enforce LF line endings for all shell scripts
*.sh text eol=lf

# Generated Lazarus resource bundles (lazres output). Pin to LF so the committed
# form is stable regardless of whether it was regenerated on Windows (lazres.exe
# writes CRLF) or Linux/macOS (LF); keeps git history from thrashing and lets the
# CI staleness check compare cleanly. See make assets / make.ps1 assets.
*.lrs text eol=lf

# CareLink login-helper sources are embedded verbatim into assets/carelink_assets.lrs
# (lazres bakes their exact bytes in as #13#10/#10 tokens). Pin to LF so the
# resource is byte-deterministic across platforms — otherwise a CRLF checkout on
# Windows regenerates a different .lrs than an LF checkout in CI.
tools/carelink-login/*.mjs text eol=lf
tools/carelink-login/*.json text eol=lf
